    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>Corpus Christi hurricane damage claims often involve three policies: a homeowners policy that excludes wind and hail, a TWIA windstorm policy, and a flood policy.</li>
<li>TWIA covers wind and hail only. Storm surge and rising water fall under flood insurance, and NFIP coverage generally has a 30-day waiting period.</li>
<li>A TWIA claim must be filed within one year of the date of damage, and TWIA has its own deadlines for decisions, payment, and disputes.</li>
<li>Before landfall: read your declarations pages, photograph your home, and gather your windstorm certificate and policy numbers.</li>
<li>After landfall: stay safe, prevent further damage, document before cleanup, report to every insurer that may be involved, and keep receipts.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>When a hurricane comes ashore anywhere near the Coastal Bend, the work of recovery starts long before the first adjuster arrives. <strong>Corpus Christi hurricane damage claims</strong> are rarely simple, because the same storm can bring roof-tearing wind, wind-driven rain, and storm surge, and each of those can fall under a different policy with its own deductible, rules, and deadlines. This guide walks through what to do before and after landfall, how the three-policy setup works for most coastal homes, and what local storm history tells us about the kinds of damage to expect.</p>

<h2>Hurricane history in Corpus Christi and the Coastal Bend</h2>

<p>The Coastal Bend has seen major hurricanes in living memory, and the damage patterns from those storms still shape how claims are handled today. A few landmark events:</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Storm</th><th>Date</th><th>Landfall facts</th><th>Claim lesson</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Hurricane Celia</td><td>Aug. 3, 1970</td><td>Category 4 at landfall in the Port Aransas / Aransas Pass area (NOAA HURDAT). NWS Corpus Christi reports 140 mph winds measured at Corpus Christi International Airport.</td><td>Extreme wind can reach well into the city, not just the islands.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hurricane Harvey</td><td>Aug. 25–26, 2017</td><td>Category 4 landfall on the northern end of San José Island, about 5 n mi east of Rockport, then a second Category 3 landfall on the northeast shore of Copano Bay (NHC).</td><td>Wind and surge damage side by side, with large numbers of homes affected across Aransas, Nueces, and San Patricio counties.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Hurricane Hanna</td><td>July 25, 2020</td><td>Category 1 landfall on Padre Island around 5 p.m. CDT (NHC). It was the first July hurricane landfall in Texas since Dolly in 2008.</td><td>Even a Category 1 storm can push surge into bayfront property and piers.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tropical Storm Harold</td><td>Aug. 22, 2023</td><td>NCEI records a 62 mph gust at Corpus Christi International Airport and elevated tides on Corpus Christi and Nueces Bays.</td><td>Tropical storms, not just hurricanes, produce fence, tree, and roof claims.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Harvey is the storm most local homeowners remember. The NOAA NCEI Storm Events Database entry for <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=720925" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nueces County</a> notes that the most significant damage in the county was in Port Aransas, where widespread major damage occurred and most homes suffered major roof damage. The <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=718519" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aransas County entry</a> describes catastrophic damage in Copano Village, Holiday Beach, and Lamar. Hanna was weaker, but the NCEI entry for <a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=912741" target="_blank" rel="noopener">coastal Nueces County</a> reports surge 2 to 4 feet above ground level that damaged parts of the Corpus Christi Marina and numerous piers along Corpus Christi Bay and inundated North Beach.</p>

<p>For a longer timeline, including lookups of events near your address, see our <a href="/storm-history/">Corpus Christi storm history page</a>.</p>

<h2>The three-policy reality for coastal homeowners</h2>

<p>Nueces, San Patricio, Aransas, and Kleberg counties are all inside the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) designated catastrophe area. In much of that area, private homeowners policies exclude wind and hail, and TWIA serves as the wind and hail insurer of last resort. TWIA’s own overview is blunt: its policies cover wind and hail losses only, and no other perils. That is why so many Coastal Bend homeowners end up with three separate policies.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>What it generally covers</th><th>What it generally does not cover</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Homeowners policy (wind/hail excluded)</td><td>Fire, theft, certain sudden water losses, liability, and other perils named in the policy</td><td>Wind and hail (excluded by endorsement), flood</td></tr>
<tr><td>TWIA windstorm policy</td><td>Direct wind and hail damage, and in many cases rain that enters through openings created by wind</td><td>Flood, storm surge, rising water, and every peril other than wind and hail</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood policy (NFIP or private)</td><td>Direct physical loss by flood, including storm surge and rising water, within policy limits</td><td>Wind damage; items and areas the flood policy limits or excludes</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Not every home fits this pattern. Some properties carry wind coverage from a private carrier, and some owners have no flood policy at all. Pull out each declarations page and confirm what you actually have. If you are not sure whether your home is TWIA-eligible, our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims page</a> explains the basics, and our guide to the <a href="/twia-claim-process-corpus-christi/">TWIA claim process</a> covers the deadlines step by step.</p>

<h2>Before landfall: what to do while there is still time</h2>

<p>Preparation is mostly about safety, but a few simple steps also make the claim that follows much easier to document.</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Read your declarations pages.</strong> Note the policy numbers, limits, and every deductible, including any wind/hail, named-storm, or hurricane deductible. These are often a percentage of the dwelling limit, which can be a large number. Our <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> shows what a percentage deductible means in dollars.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph and video your property.</strong> Walk every room, open closets, and record the roof, exterior walls, fences, and outbuildings from the ground. Date-stamped “before” images are some of the most useful evidence you will ever have.</li>
<li><strong>Find your windstorm certificate.</strong> If your home was built or improved on or after Jan. 1, 1988, TWIA eligibility generally depends on a certificate of compliance, often called a WPI-8. Keep a copy with your policy papers. See our article on <a href="/windstorm-certificate-wpi-8-insurance-claims/">windstorm certificates and WPI-8</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Check flood coverage early.</strong> NFIP coverage generally starts 30 days after purchase, with limited exceptions. If a storm is already in the Gulf, it is usually too late for that storm.</li>
<li><strong>Store documents and contacts off-site.</strong> Save policy PDFs, agent contacts, and insurer claim lines to your phone or cloud storage in case you evacuate.</li>
<li><strong>Secure what you can safely.</strong> Bring in loose items, close shutters, and follow local evacuation orders. Nothing in a claim is worth staying in harm’s way.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our <a href="/hurricane-season-guide/">hurricane season guide</a> has a fuller preparation checklist.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>After landfall: the first days of a hurricane claim</h2>

<h3>1. Put safety first</h3>
<p>Wait for officials to say it is safe to return. Watch for downed power lines, gas odors, standing water that may be energized, and weakened structures. Do not climb onto a damaged roof.</p>

<h3>2. Prevent further damage</h3>
<p>Most policies require reasonable steps to protect the property from more damage. That usually means tarping roof openings, boarding broken windows, removing standing water, and starting drying. Keep receipts for materials, equipment rental, and contractor invoices, and photograph the conditions before and after each step.</p>

<h3>3. Document before cleanup</h3>
<p>Take wide shots and close-ups of every damaged area. Photograph water lines on walls and furniture, debris left behind by water, missing shingles or panels, displaced flashing, and interior ceiling stains. Keep samples of damaged materials where practical. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lists what adjusters typically look for.</p>

<h3>4. Report to every insurer that may be involved</h3>
<p>If the storm brought both wind and water, report to your windstorm insurer and your flood insurer, and notify your homeowners carrier if any other covered peril is involved. Write down each claim number, the date you reported, and the name of every person you speak with.</p>

<h3>5. Track your deadlines</h3>
<p>For TWIA, the claim must be filed no later than one year after the date of damage. TWIA may request missing information within 30 days of filing and must accept or deny in writing within 60 days after it receives the claim or the requested information, whichever is later. Private carriers in Texas generally follow the Prompt Payment of Claims Act in Chapter 542 of the Insurance Code, and those deadlines are extended 15 days after a declared weather-related catastrophe. Write each date down as it happens so you can keep them straight.</p>

<h3>6. Prepare for the adjuster inspection</h3>
<p>Have your photos, receipts, and a written list of damage ready. Walk the adjuster through every area, including attic spaces, exterior siding, fencing, and outbuildings. Ask how the adjuster is separating wind damage from water damage, and ask for a copy of the estimate when it is issued.</p>

<h2>Common problems in Corpus Christi hurricane damage claims</h2>

<h3>Wind vs. water disputes</h3>
<p>When surge or rising water and wind both reach a home, each insurer may point to the other. The windstorm carrier may attribute interior damage to flood, while the flood carrier may say the water came through a wind-damaged roof. Evidence of where water entered, how high it rose, and when the damage occurred matters a great deal. Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-storm-surge-damage-claims-texas-coast/">wind vs. storm surge damage claims</a> covers this in detail.</p>

<h3>Incomplete roof and exterior scopes</h3>
<p>Hurricane estimates sometimes count only the most obvious damage, leaving out items like underlayment, flashing, drip edge, ridge and hip components, gutters, or damage on less visible slopes. Composition shingle, metal, and low-slope roofs each have their own repair considerations.</p>

<h3>Code upgrades and windstorm inspection</h3>
<p>Corpus Christi enforces local design criteria for wind, and roof repairs in the catastrophe area generally require TDI windstorm inspection. Code-required items are paid only to the extent the policy includes ordinance or law coverage, so it is worth checking that limit before a storm. Our article on <a href="/windstorm-certificate-wpi-8-insurance-claims/">windstorm certificates</a> explains how inspection fits into repairs.</p>

<h3>Depreciation and payment timing</h3>
<p>Many policies pay actual cash value first and release recoverable depreciation after repairs are completed, often within a deadline set in the policy. Keep your contractor invoices and proof of completion so you can request that holdback.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warn"><span class="callout-title">Watch for “sign today” pressure</span><p>After a major storm, door-to-door contractors and others may push for immediate signatures. Read every document before signing, and remember that anyone acting as a public adjuster in Texas must hold a license from the Texas Department of Insurance.</p></div>

<h2>How a public adjuster can help</h2>

<p>A licensed public adjuster represents the policyholder only. On a hurricane loss, that work can include reading each policy, documenting wind, water, and contents damage, preparing a detailed estimate, and handling communication with the windstorm, flood, and homeowners carriers so the pieces fit together. Corpus Christi Public Adjuster is a service of Rise Public Adjusting LLC (TDI license #3356839), serving Corpus Christi, Nueces County, and surrounding Coastal Bend communities, including <a href="/service-areas/port-aransas-tx/">Port Aransas</a> and Rockport.</p>

<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Texas law also gives you 72 hours after signing to rescind a public adjuster contract by written notice. A public adjuster is not an attorney; if your claim involves a lawsuit, bad faith allegations, or a deadline to sue, talk to a Texas attorney.</p>

